.TH "setup-ltsp-root" "8" "2026-09-11" "ltsp6"
.SH "NAME"
setup\-ltsp\-root \(em Configure the image server: NBD, TFTP, proxy DHCP and PXE
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-root\fR \fIown\-ip\fR \fIiface\fR \fIgateway\fR [\fIdns\fR ...]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Run on the image server, as root. Sets up the nbd\-server export, dnsmasq for
proxy DHCP plus PXE plus TFTP, the checksum offload workaround, and rewrites
\fBnbdroot=\fR in the iPXE boot script to point at this host.
.PP
This machine builds and serves the client image and answers PXE. It runs no
user sessions, and the application servers build no images \(em that separation
is deliberate and is what keeps an image rebuild from disturbing anybody's
desktop.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
.IP "\fIown\-ip\fP" 4
This host's address. Written into the iPXE script's \fBnbdroot=\fR, so it must
be the address clients can reach.
.IP "\fIiface\fP" 4
Interface to configure, for example ens18.
.IP "\fIgateway\fP" 4
Default gateway.
.IP "\fIdns\fP" 4
One or more resolvers, handed to PXE clients as \fBoption:dns\-server\fR.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.PP
.nf
setup\-ltsp\-root 172.17.250.20 ens18 172.17.240.1 172.17.250.10
.fi
.SH "FILES"
.PP
.IP "\fI/etc/nbd\-server/config\fP" 4
Holds a \fB[generic]\fR stanza with an \fBincludedir\fR plus one stanza per
image. Several named exports share one port, which is how thin and fat images
are served side by side:
.PP
.nf
    [thin\-amd64]
        exportname = /srv/ltsp/images/thin\-amd64.img
        readonly = true

    [fat\-amd64]
        exportname = /srv/ltsp/images/fat\-amd64.img
        readonly = true
.fi
.IP "\fI/srv/tftp/ltsp.ipxe\fP" 4
The boot script. \fBtoram\fR copies the image into tmpfs and disconnects NBD,
so a booted client survives the image server going away. Confirm it took
effect on a client with \fBfindmnt | grep \-E 'toram|rofs'\fR and
\fBdmesg | grep \-i nbd_disconnect\fR.
.IP "\fI/etc/dnsmasq.d/ltsp.conf\fP" 4
Proxy DHCP, PXE and TFTP.
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
Proxy DHCP, not a second authoritative server. The segment already has a DHCP
server; running another one that also hands out addresses is how you get two
servers answering the same request. In proxy mode dnsmasq answers only the PXE
part and leaves addressing alone.
.PP
\fBport=0\fR keeps dnsmasq out of DNS entirely, which is correct here because
the domain controller owns the zone \(em but it also means dnsmasq advertises no
resolver at all, and the Debian installer then stalls at \(lqName server
addresses\(rq. Pass the \fIdns\fR argument so \fBoption:dns\-server\fR is sent
explicitly.
.PP
Checksum offload is disabled through a systemd unit. Under nested
virtualisation there is no hardware left to finish the checksum, so DHCP
replies leave invalid and every client drops them; the symptom is PXE clients
that never get an address while a capture on this side shows the reply being
sent. The unit needs \fBethtool\fR installed \(em without the binary it is
enabled, visible and permanently \fIfailed\fR. Check the state, not the
intent:
.PP
.nf
systemctl is\-active ltsp\-nic\-offload
ethtool \-k \fIiface\fR | grep tx\-checksumming
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBbuild\-autoinstall\-iso\fP(8),
\fBltsp\-update\-image\fP(8),
\fBnbd\-server\fP(1),
\fBdnsmasq\fP(8),
/usr/share/doc/ltsp\-server/NBD\-toram,
/usr/share/doc/ltsp\-server/PXE\-network\-boot\-test.
.SH "AVAILABILITY"
.PP
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-root\fR is part of the \fBltsp\-server\fP package; the latest
version is available from \fIhttps://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6\fR.
